WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.720
"The Role Meat May Play in
Triggering Parkinson's Disease"

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.960
Parkinson's disease is an ever-worsening
neurodegenerative disorder that results

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.720
in death and affects about 1 in 50
of us when we get older.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.760
A small minority of cases are
genetic and run in families,

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.880
but 85 to 90% of cases are sporadic,
meaning they just seemingly pop up

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.010
out of nowhere.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.400
It's caused by the death of a certain
kind of nerve cell in the brain.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.560
Once about 70% of them
are gone, the symptoms start.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.520
Okay, so what kills off those cells?
It's still not completely clear,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.280
but the abnormal clumping of a protein
called alpha-synuclein (α-synuclein)

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.840
is thought to be involved. Why?
Because if you inject blended

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.520
Parkinson's brains into the heads
of rats or monkeys, you can induce

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.680
Parkinson's pathology and symptoms,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.480
or even just injecting the pure clumped
α-synuclein strands themselves.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.600
Okay, but how do these clumps
naturally end up in the brain?

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.880
It all seems to start in the gut.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.120
The part of the brain where the pathology
often first shows up is directly connected

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.920
to the gut, and we have direct
evidence of the spread

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.480
of Parkinson's pathology from the
gastrointestinal tract to the brain.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.040
Alpha-synuclein from brains
of Parkinson's patients

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.560
is taken up in the gut wall,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.400
and creeping up the vagal nerves
from the gut into the brain.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.480
But this was in rats. If only
we had a way to go back

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.560
and look at people's colons
before they got Parkinson's.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.520
And indeed, we can. Old colon
biopsies were dredged up

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.840
from people who had later
developed Parkinson's,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.360
and years before symptoms arose, you
could see the α-synuclein in their gut.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.480
Research supported by the Michael J. Fox
Foundation has found

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.000
you can reliably distinguish
the colons of patients from controls

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.960
by the presence of the Parkinson's
protein lodged in the gut wall.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.160
But how did it get there
in the first place?

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.600
Perhaps vertebrate food products

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.840
as a potential source
of prion-like α-synuclein.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.840
See, nearly all of the animals
with back bones that we eat--

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.880
cows, chickens, pigs, and fish--
express the protein α-synuclein.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.840
And so when we eat common
meat products, when we eat

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.960
skeletal muscle, we're
eating nerves, blood cells,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.720
and the muscle cells themselves.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.360
Every pound of meat has up to
like a teaspoon of blood in it

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.000
and that alone could be
an α-synuclein source

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.160
to potentially trigger
a clumping cascade

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.320
of our own α-synuclein in the gut.
Though it may seem intuitive

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.400
that dietary α-synuclein could seed
this kind of buildup in our gut,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.200
what evidence do we have
that it's actually happening?

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.440
These are pretty interesting data.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.560
There's a surgical procedure
called a vagotomy

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.080
in which the big nerve that goes
from your gut to your brain is cut

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.960
as an old-timey treatment
for stomach ulcers.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.280
Would cutting communication between the
gut and the brain reduce Parkinson's risk?

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.720
Apparently so, suggesting that
the gut to brain vagal nerve

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.840
may be critically involved in the
development of Parkinson's disease.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.960
Now, of course, many people regularly
consume meat and dairy products,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.680
but only a small fraction of the general
population will develop Parkinson's.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.800
So there must be other factors at
play that may somehow provide

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.920
an opportunity for unwanted
dietary α-synuclein

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.800
to enter the host and initiate disease.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.600
For example, your gut becomes leakier
as you age so might that play a role?

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.320
Well, what else makes your gut leaky?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.440
Dietary fiber deprivation has also been
shown to degrade the intestinal barrier

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.760
and enhance pathogen entry.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.520
So this all raises possibilities
for food-based therapy.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.080
Parkinson's patients have significantly
less Prevotella in their gut, a friendly

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.360
fiber-eating flora that bolsters
your intestinal barrier function.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.840
So low levels of Prevotella
are linked to a leaky gut,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.440
which has been linked to
intestinal α-synuclein deposition.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.200
But fiber-rich foods may
bring Prevotella levels back up.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.080
Therefore, by adopting
a plant-based diet,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.720
in addition to getting the beneficial
effects of phytonutrients,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.320
it's possible that increasing
overall fiber intake may modify

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.560
the gut microbiome and
gut leakiness in beneficial ways.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.320
So, does a vegan diet, one
with lots of fiber and no meat,

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.280
reduce risk for Parkinson's disease?

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.520
Parkinson's does appear to
be rare in quasi-vegan cultures.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.240
Rates are about five times lower in rural
sub-Saharan Africa for instance.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.040
Now all this time we were thinking
the benefits seen for Parkinson's

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.760
from plant-based diets
was due to the antioxidants

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.040
and anti-inflammatory nature
of the animal-free diets,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.160
but maybe it's also due to the increased
intestinal exposure to fiber and

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:02.546
decreased intestinal exposure to
ingested nerves, muscles, and blood.

